00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this morning the mining project that is  either critical to america's clean  energy future or a pending mess in the  american west depending on who you speak  with the mining site in question idaho  has a long and storied history  now it's back in the news as a new  company seeks to reopen the mine that  hasn't been used in more than two  decades they believe they can remove  billions of dollars of gold buried in  the ground while fixing damage done in  the past and increasing national  security  not everyone is buying  our trip to the mining site in central  idaho took us through some of the  wildest land in the country over  mountains and past landslides  eventually arriving in remote yellow  pine and stib night for 150 years miners  have come here seeking to dig their  fortune the latest arrival perpetua  resources  laurel sayer is the ceo we still need  gold in society today it's not just used  for jewelry we use it in electronics  it's a very valuable mineral that we use  the stem night mine was abandoned in the  1990s  but a toxic legacy was left behind  changes to the river that flows through  the mine site meant fish spawning  upstream were left without clear passage  to the pacific ocean  perpetua says they can clean up the mess  left behind by for one reconnecting the  river the first thing we do is pick up  hundreds of thousands of yards of tails  and move those so that those are no  longer contaminating the site plus they  say there's another big reason to dig  antimony a chemical element that's a  byproduct of the gold extraction process  and something that's crucial for the  production of green technology including  batteries for electric vehicles  in the 1940s antimony mined here and  used in the production of ammunition  helped the u.s win world war ii  today the vast majority is produced in  china and russia none in the u.s which  some say is a national security risk  if we sit and do nothing  we will never solve this problem we want  to do something now because we're  already behind so if there is an  opportunity  to help advance the clean energy agenda  for the u.s we should be doing it  not everyone agrees  the 13.2 million acres that make up the  section of idaho where the mine is  located was the original home of a  native american tribe known as the nez  purse or namipu just probably 40 miles  outside of spokane you're in our  traditional territory nakia williamson  feels after losing their land of the  gold rush of the 1800s the namipu have  given enough we had to sustain so much  loss already with many other impacts  that have happened to the land the other  transformation of this landscape and one  more impact could  be too much for for us to sustain it's  an enormous mine and it's going to  increase almost uh over two times the  size as it is now  right down there emmett taylor is  another tribal leader who we met at the  south fork of the salmon river which  once contained the most thriving  population of chinook salmon in the  world remember my niece catching her  first salmon here lyanna oh yeah yeah  today because of pollutants like those  released from mining operations in the  past  overfishing and dams that block fish  migration it's estimated only one  percent of the salmon that used to run  this section of the river remain our  whole goal is to try to  restore the fishery bring the fishery  back  to get the  salmon back to a sustainable level  from it for an ecological standpoint and  also from a harvest standpoint  it's a complex issue they mean there's a  lot of  a lot of stakeholders the namipu are not  the only ones pushing back on the  perpetua plan  nick nilsson is the executive director  of idaho rivers united if this project  happens  a generation from now  what do you think this portion of the  country looks like  i see a huge scar at the  headwaters of the south fork of the  salmon  you know i see  what could be a sterile river with the  mining company and the area that they'll  be working in and all the different  conditions that are there it's not a  question of  if a spill is ever going to occur or if  an accident's ever going to occur it's  um it's a matter of when  perpetua does have many supporters  including politicians and locals lynn  immel has lived in the tiny town of  yellow pine near the mine since 1968.  how old is this crucible  this is probably from the late 1800s  the prospect of new jobs and a  reconnected river is intriguing for  those like her who remain i'm totally in  favor of the mine  and the mess that is left  has to be cleaned up there are some who  feel there's going to be a whole new  mess  left behind after i think the  regulations will totally control it you  think  government's not going to clean that up  but you think private  industry can  i think they will how long would the  mining last for 15 years we have always  been transparent about who we are and  what we're doing up here that we're a  modern mining project what is a modern  mining project mean  we put in for example the restoration  part of our project  we're actually restoring habitat for the  fish and the critters that they can  thrive up here once we're done and it's  even before we're done if their plan  and the science were as good as their pr  were then i'd have a lot less concerns  about it but  it really isn't at some point we have to  shift away from fossil fuels move away  from them  extracting metals like this allows that  to happen why is that a bad idea this  isn't an anti-extraction or an  anti-mining campaign it's  you know mining needs to be done we all  recognize that but it needs to be done  responsibly and there are certain  locations that it's never going to be  responsible  last year the environmental protection  agency centered had significant concerns  about harm to fish and that water  treatment at the site would be needed  forever  perpetual revised that plant and said  they'll shrink the mine's footprint and  lessen the impact on the environments  the review process of that new plan has  just been extended for another two years  idaho rivers united and the namipu  consider that a victory  for now we are accountable to this land  and we are the voice for this land and  so it's our responsibility to continue  to advocate for those that no longer  have the voice and has been imparted to  us and that's the responsibility that we  carry as namibu do you believe there's a  compromise here  i really struggle to find a compromise  here from what they want to do  when i asked the tribe if they thought  there was any room for compromise  they said no  can you do this project without them i  would hope that they wouldn't say no  forever and i would hope that they would  come to the table and we could  fix this  the process the permitting process does  allow us to move forward  but our door is always open  we want to find a compromise we want to  work with them  such beautiful land and such a tough  debate no matter what happens you're  talking long term here if it gets  approved you're talking years of  construction then 15 to 20 years of  mining um and i think a lot of people  are just wondering whatever happens here  what this area is going to look like a  generation from now and i love that the  tribe speaks for the land you want them  on your side you don't want to do  without them yeah and that this is  playing out though in a public way i  think that's so important that this  discussion is actually taking place  whereas this is something that didn't  always happen as we well know in our  history in the past transparency yeah  thanks jeff good story

